}_{2}\\cdot {x}_{3}$, where ${s}_{4}={x}_{1}{s}_{1}+{x}_{2}{s}_{2}+{x}_{3}{s}_{3}.$ \n\n$$\nS=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & -5 & 5 \\\\ -2 & -1 & 10 & -1 \\\\ -1 & -2 & 5 & 4\\end{aligned}]\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we reduce the matrix $S$ to reduced row echelon form (RREF):\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & -5 & 5 \\\\ -2 & -1 & 10 & -1 \\\\ -1 & -2 & 5 & 4\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+2{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & -5 & 5 \\\\ 0 & -3 & 0 & 9 \\\\ -1 & -2 & 5 & 4\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & -5 & 5 \\\\ 0 & -3 & 0 & 9 \\\\ 0 & -3 & 0 & 9\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :=(-\\frac{1}{3}){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & -5 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & -3 \\\\ 0 & -3 & 0 & 9\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+3{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & -5 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the matrix above, we see that the pivot columns are the $1$st and $2$nd columns. So, a basis of $\\text{Col}(S)$ is ${{s}_{1},{s}_{2}}.$ \n\nTherefore, we conclude that $B={{s}_{1},{s}_{2},{s}_{3}}$ is not a basis of $\\text{Col}(S).$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["q-111809/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c3bf9ea3-d57f-53f6-aa3c-eddffe1a8cfa"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-111809"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-111516/selection/16979b9fcaf7be4d0f90e7671616b0d917ebde732b6de51a145392907f0915fa" :answer/id #uuid "cfb72294-14e6-5263-a985-fc8357625a42" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "${{r}_{1},{r}_{2},{r}_{3}}$ is not a basis of $\\text{Col}(R)$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-111516/selection/b13ac151352392d38026eed875b911708c50c896d6c09463410ac041030163ff" :answer/id #uuid "1756de60-af11-54be-914a-cca901e082a2" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-111516/selection/0f0db3a18f1d79c4863315eefe754cc5306e04e7aeebf4414ffedb95d6f1bc97" :answer/id #uuid "9211832d-2078-5e60-8642-041acc4484b2" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "12" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-111516/selection/a0e0e68f25b8b46aafef3b74aff7beff7fddc6de5bf36921680b04c7b0e6ffaa" :answer/id #uuid "98e64578-fc4e-5f07-a0be-6378b7725f1d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "6" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-111516/selection/ad06fa8bb7e77edd4098e00237718855c96ba589745d004ae1aed5be17e6549c" :answer/id #uuid "dc9b4c50-87fb-5cba-a2d5-144823e7d799" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-111516/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "37f70761-9ab2-5bd3-8376-f021983820fc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-111516/selection/16979b9fcaf7be4d0f90e7671616b0d917ebde732b6de51a145392907f0915fa" :answer-field/choices ["q-111516/selection/16979b9fcaf7be4d0f90e7671616b0d917ebde732b6de51a145392907f0915fa" "q-111516/selection/b13ac151352392d38026eed875b911708c50c896d6c09463410ac041030163ff" "q-111516/selection/0f0db3a18f1d79c4863315eefe754cc5306e04e7aeebf4414ffedb95d6f1bc97" "q-111516/selection/a0e0e68f25b8b46aafef3b74aff7beff7fddc6de5bf36921680b04c7b0e6ffaa" "q-111516/selection/ad06fa8bb7e77edd4098e00237718855c96ba589745d004ae1aed5be17e6549c"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-111516" :question/id #uuid "5f497921-6832-52d1-b124-1ae1522c9728" :question/math-academy-id "q-111516" :question/problem "The matrix $R$ is given below. Let ${r}_{1}$, ${r}_{2}$,  ${r}_{3}$ and ${r}_{4}$ be the respective columns of $R.$\nDetermine if \n$B=\\text{{}{r}_{1},{r}_{2},{r}_{3}\\text{}}$ is a basis of $\\text{Col}(R).$ \nIf that is the case, find ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}\\cdot {x}_{3}$, where ${r}_{4}={x}_{1}{r}_{1}+{x}_{2}{r}_{2}+{x}_{3}{r}_{3}.$ \n\n$$\nR=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 3 & -2 & 1 & -3 \\\\ 12 & -6 & 6 & -6\\end{aligned}]\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we reduce the matrix $R$ to reduced row echelon form (RREF):\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 3 & -2 & 1 & -3 \\\\ 12 & -6 & 6 & -6\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-3){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -2 & -2 & -6 \\\\ 12 & -6 & 6 & -6\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+(-12){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -2 & -2 & -6 \\\\ 0 & -6 & -6 & -18\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :=(-\\frac{1}{2}){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & -6 & -6 & -18\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+6{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the matrix above, we see that the pivot columns are the $1$st and $2$nd columns. So, a basis of $\\text{Col}(R)$ is ${{r}_{1},{r}_{2}}.$ \n\nTherefore, we conclude that $B={{r}_{1},{r}_{2},{r}_{3}}$ is not a basis of $\\text{Col}(R).$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["q-111516/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c3bf9ea3-d57f-53f6-aa3c-eddffe1a8cfa"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-111516"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-6254"] :question/problem "Consider the matrix ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14066433/assets/81c99a47d72cc5e259acc8b4b33bf58c71c5d20e6c695beeeb71e0c784b614d5.png) Find a basis $\\mathcal B$ of $\\textrm{Col}(A).$" :question/worked-solution "In order to find a basis of $\\textrm{Col}(A),$ we need reduce the matrix $A$ to row echelon form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 3 & 2 & 5\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-3){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & -4 & 8\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe pivot columns correspond to linearly independent vectors, while the non-pivot columns correspond to linearly dependent vectors that can be removed. Here, the $1$st and $2$nd columns are pivot columns, while the $3$rd column is a non-pivot column.\n\nRemember that we need to pick the pivot columns from the *original matrix*, not from the reduced one. \n\nThe $1$st and $2$nd columns from the original matrix are $\\mathbf{a}_1$ and $\\mathbf{a}_2.$ So, a basis of $\\textrm{Col}(A)$ is $\\{ \\mathbf{a}_1, \\mathbf{a}_2 \\},$ that is to say, \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14066433/assets/02a95bd3513fcd70ea74936f330472e5bf5a1e14b189da9c30c5876782d12632.png)" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-6255"] :question/problem "The matrix $A$ is given below. Let $\\mathbf{a}_1$, $\\mathbf{a}_2$, and $\\mathbf{a}_3$ be the respective columns of $A.$ Determine if ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14066433/assets/f7e27a29b580edc016bf479e983cb17c5e61ebb800b03e671b89b5d556056533.png) is a basis of $\\textrm{Col}(A).$ \nIf that is the case, find $x_1 \\cdot x_2$, where $\\mathbf{a}_3=x_1\\mathbf{a}_1+x_2\\mathbf{a}_2.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14066433/assets/cce279327716181e5b5c5c1e95e7eac34c2b93a78a1f7e773393ffbf608cb1f4.png)" :question/worked-solution "First, we reduce the matrix $A$ to reduced row echelon form (RREF):\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 9 \\\\ 3 & 1 & 17\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-3){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 9 \\\\ 0 & -5 & -10\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :=(-\\frac{1}{5}){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 9 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+(-2){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the matrix above, we see that the pivot columns are the $1$st and $2$nd columns. So, a basis of $\\textrm{Col}(A)$ is $\\{\\mathbf{a}_1,\\mathbf{a}_2 \\},$ that is to say,\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14066433/assets/f083a27cf3de0a6150020b6f5887096258880d4f5e30de1cfb3bb091b79a16f9.png)\n\nNow, since the $3$rd column of the reduced matrix is a non-pivot column, then the entries in that column tell us the coefficients needed to express $\\mathbf{a}_3$ as a linear combination of all the other columns in the original matrix. So, we have\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14066433/assets/96f39576ea8196a88248035262eb7cd006c134717f695b23c575667b6562f6e0.png)\n\nThis gives us $x_1 \\cdot x_2 = 5 \\cdot 2=10.$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-6272"] :question/problem "The matrix $A$ is given below. Let $\\mathbf{a}_1$, $\\mathbf{a}_2$,  $\\mathbf{a}_3$, and $\\mathbf{a}_4$ be the respective columns of $A.$ Determine if ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14066433/assets/f7e27a29b580edc016bf479e983cb17c5e61ebb800b03e671b89b5d556056533.png) is a basis of $\\textrm{Col}(A).$ If that is the case, find $x_1 \\cdot x_2$, where $\\mathbf{a}_4=x_1\\mathbf{a}_1+x_2\\mathbf{a}_2.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14066433/assets/70f59cf06f15bb416d32ffa9b28d604f4a842273ac8bd04d9f59b121ca1dc40d.png)" :question/worked-solution "First, we reduce the matrix $A$ to reduced row echelon form (RREF):\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 & -4 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -6 & -3 \\\\ 1 & 2 & -14 & -4\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :=\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & -2 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -6 & -3 \\\\ 1 & 2 & -14 & -4\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+(-1){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & -2 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -6 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -12 & -6\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+(-2){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & -2 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -6 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the matrix above, we see that the pivot columns are the $1$st and $2$nd columns. So, a basis of $\\textrm{Col}(A)$ is $\\{\\mathbf{a}_1,\\mathbf{a}_2 \\},$ that is to say,\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14066433/assets/9c6ae7fc8b0c044bf2286ea2b9d41c7a427deebf8184a3515ad2b20edc682afc.png)\n\nNow, since the $4$th column of the reduced matrix is a non-pivot column, then the entries in that column tell us the coefficients needed to express $\\mathbf{a}_4$ as a linear combination of all the other columns in the original matrix. So, we have\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14066433/assets/4a0d70f028211fafeab4353e5c5e34cb1895be2350f5fd0211b6e76856dea3e0.png)\n\nThis gives us $x_1 \\cdot x_2 = 2 \\cdot (-3) = -6.$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]}]
